      "id": "ap",
      "label": "Down the pathway",
      "at": {
        "node": "AP_left_free_wall_atrial",
        "wave": 0
      },
      "caption": "Unlike the AV node, the pathway has no decremental delay and conducts fast impulses. It carries many atrial impulses straight to the ventricle.",
      "ecg": "Wide QRS complexes (pre-excited)."
    },
    {
      "id": "fast",
      "label": "Fast and irregular",
      "at": {
        "firstTissue": "ventricular",
        "wave": 2
      },
      "caption": "The ventricular rate can be much faster than in ordinary atrial fibrillation, and the complexes vary as the AV node and pathway share the work.",
      "ecg": "A fast, irregular wide-complex rhythm."
    },
    {
      "id": "danger",
      "label": "Risk of VF",
      "at": {
        "lastTissue": "ventricular",
        "wave": 15
      },
      "caption": "Very rapid conduction over the pathway can degenerate into ventricular fibrillation. AV-node-blocking drugs can make this worse by pushing more impulses down the pathway.",
      "ecg": "Irregular wide complexes at very short R-R intervals."
    }
  ],
  "teaching_points": [
    "In AF with pre-excitation the accessory pathway can conduct very fast, so the ventricular rate is rapid and irregular (slides 85-86).",
    "It can degenerate into ventricular fibrillation (slide 87).",
    "AV nodal blocking drugs are avoided because they do not slow the pathway."
  ]
